data.content:
Here's what changed and how to fix each piece:

### 1. Namespaces moved
```csharp
// Remove these:
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Any;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;
// Replace with:
using Microsoft.OpenApi;
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
```

### 2. `OpenApiString` removed — use `JsonNode`
```csharp
// Before
param.Schema.Example = new OpenApiString("1.0");
// After
param.Schema.Example = JsonNode.Parse("\"1.0\"");
```

### 3. `OpenApiSecurityScheme.Reference` removed — use `OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference`
```csharp
// Before
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = "oauth2" }
};
// After
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference("oauth2", null);
```

### 4. Collections may be null — add null checks
```csharp
operation.Responses ??= new OpenApiResponses();
operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });
// Also use ?. for Parameters:
var param = operation.Parameters?.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Name == "api-version");
```

### 5. `Schema.Nullable` removed
OpenAPI 3.1 uses JSON Schema's `type: ["string", "null"]` instead of `nullable: true`. Remove the `.Nullable = false` assignment entirely — it no longer exists on `OpenApiSchema`.

### Summary
The root cause is `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0` pulling in **Microsoft.OpenApi v2.x**, which restructured all namespaces, replaced `OpenApiAny` types with `JsonNode`, introduced `OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference`, made collections nullable, and dropped `Nullable` in favor of JSON Schema 3.1 semantics.
